{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Of Benjamin he said.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): concerning Benjamin he said that he is the beloved of YHWH — 'Benjamin shall dwell securely relying on HaShem, and HaShem dwells between his shoulders.'" "<b>Hovering over him.</b> Over the Glory that dwelt in Jerusalem, in the portion of Benjamin. And the sense of 'between his shoulders' and not 'upon his head' is because the Temple is not at the very peak of the mountain but slightly lower down, twenty-three cubits below the spring of Etam, and David's intention was to build it there, as is expounded in the chapter on Slaughter of Consecrated Animals, and so Rashi, of blessed memory, explained. And note that Moses here called it by the expression 'shoulder,' and likewise Joshua his disciple called it thus (Joshua 15:8): אל כתף היבוסי, and the reason it is called Jebusite is because David acquired it from Araunah the Jebusite. And some explained the expression 'between his shoulders' to mean because it is in the middle of his inheritance." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: this verse contains an allusion to three Temples. 'Shall dwell securely' — this is the First Temple, and therefore the language of dwelling is mentioned in connection with it. 'Hovering over him all the day' — this is the Second Temple, and it did not say 'dwell' but only 'hover,' because the Divine Presence did not abide in it constantly. 'Between his shoulders He dwells' — this is the Third Temple, when the Divine Presence will return to the Holy of Holies. Thus did the Sages, of blessed memory, expound in the Sifrei. And all this is alluded to in the blessing of Benjamin, for his merit and his honor, that he was the host of the Divine Presence. And they said, of blessed memory, concerning him that the worm did not rule over him; and this is what they said at the end of the chapter HaShutafin: 'Our Rabbis taught: Seven over whom the worm and maggot did not rule, and these are they: Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob; Moses, Aaron, and Miriam; and Benjamin son of Jacob. Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob — because it is written of them: בכל מכל כל. Moses, Aaron, and Miriam — because it is written of them: על פי ה׳; but in the case of Miriam, על פי ה׳ was not stated, for the matter would be degrading to say, but rather it is derived from Moses. Benjamin son of Jacob — as it is written of him: ידיד ה׳ ישכון לבטח עליו. Some say: also David, as it is stated (Psalms 16): אף בשרי ישכון לבטח — end of quotation.' And I heard a support for this: (Exodus 16:24) ולא הבאיש ורמה לא היתה בו — read it as: איש רמה לא היתה בו, and this is an asmakhta for the opinion of Rabbi Elazar; and the word בו is an allusion and asmakhta for the opinion of 'some say,' for David was the eighth." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'Of Benjamin he said: the beloved of YHWH' — that is the Glory; 'shall dwell upon Benjamin.' And he said לבטח ('securely'), because through this he would trust in the Great Name that is within him, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 14:31): וירא ישראל את היד הגדולה, ויאמינו בה'. And on account of this, Solomon was called by the name ידידיה." "<b>And thus</b> they expounded in the Book Bahir: The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Solomon: 'Since your name is called by the name of My Glory, I will give you My daughter,' as it is written (1 Kings 5:26): וה׳ נתן חכמה לשלמה כאשר דבר לו — and it did not specify; and where did He specify? Further on (ibid. 3): כי ראו כי חכמת אלהים בקרבו לעשות משפט — end of quotation. And we learn from all this that there is none among all the tribes blessed like Benjamin in the indwelling of the Divine Presence in his portion, and in merit and privilege, and he has superiority over all the rest of his brothers. And perhaps for this reason Scripture mentioned him on his own and said לבנימן without a vav, for He did not wish to intermingle him and include his blessing with those of the rest of the tribes, for all of them are bound together by the binding of the vav. And further, according to this approach it is possible to say that the reason Scripture mentioned 'לבנימן' of all the brothers and mentioned him separated on his own without a vav, is in order to allude to his paradigm, namely that he is part of the structure yet stands apart from them on his own, in the manner of the etrog, and this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {2 "The asmakhta on Exodus 16:24 rests on reading the unvocalized letters ורמה as either ורמה ('and a maggot') or איש רמה ('a man of maggots'), a classic midrashic letter-level play; preserved as he writes it. The Talmudic passage cited is b. Bava Batra 17a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:22.031324145Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}